{
  "requirement": "Usage MUST record mandatory input counts and sizes by evidence kind, in UTF-8\nbytes and lines, and the same information for additional inputs requested through\n`habeas` or `ripgrep`. A requirements input MUST also record its number of matched declarations.\nUsage MUST distinguish unique inputs from repeated reads, successful from\nfailed reads, and prepared inputs from those actually sent to the model.\nFile-discovery calls MUST count as tool calls without recording directory\nlistings as evidence or source inputs.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the requirement is met.** The code marked {impl} covers every obligation, and the tests cover the main cases and boundaries. I found no acknowledged gaps, so there is no FIXME.\n\n**Implementation**\n- **Sizes and kinds.** `UsageLog::prepared` in `src/authoring/usage.rs` records one `InputUsage` per prepared input. It stores the `kind`, which is `outline`, `requirements` or `definition` and matches the evidence-input kinds used by `habeas`, `ripgrep` and the mandatory bundle. It also stores UTF-8 `bytes` (the summed `str::len` of the text lines plus the newline separators) and `lines`. The `origin` field is `mandatory`, `habeas` or `ripgrep`. `configured_record` in `src/authoring/mod.rs` prepares the mandatory inputs. `assess` prepares each tool delivery under the tool's name, so mandatory and additional inputs get the same information. I read the kind-by-kind requirement as per-input records tagged with `kind`, with no aggregate totals per kind. That reading is reasonable, but it is a judgment call.\n- **Requirements inputs.** `components` is the length of the `matches` array, so a requirements input records its number of matched declarations. Other inputs record 1.\n- **Unique vs repeated.** `unique` is set by `seen.insert(input.to_string())`, so a re-delivered identical input is marked `unique: false`.\n- **Successful vs failed reads.** `UsageLog::tool` increments `tool_calls` and `tool_failures` per tool name. A failed read adds no input, because `assess` calls `prepared` only on `Ok(delivery)`.\n- **Prepared vs sent.** `begin_request` stamps `submitted_in_request` on every prepared input. `end_request` stamps `sent_in_request` only when the provider returned usage, so only completed requests count. A failed attempt leaves `sent_in_request` unset, and a retry sets it.\n- **File discovery.** `list_files` and `outline` return `ToolDelivery { inputs: Vec::new() }`. `assess` still calls `usage.tool(name, false)` for them, so they count as tool calls but add no input. The `list_files` code itself is marked for `file_discovery` rather than `usage_inputs`. The usage-side accounting that implements this obligation is marked correctly.\n- **Marking.** `configured_record`, `assess`, `InputUsage`, `prepared`, `begin_request`, `end_request`, `tool` and `skipped_tool` all carry `{impl}` for `usage_inputs`. I found no other unmarked code that implements part of the requirement.\n\n**Tests**\n- `input_metadata_counts_components_and_reads_without_persisting_source` (`usage.rs`):\n  - checks kind and the UTF-8 byte count with a multibyte `λ`;\n  - checks lines, `components == 2` for a requirements input and the `unique` true/false distinction;\n  - checks the `origin`, and the null → submitted → sent progression of `submitted_in_request` and `sent_in_request`;\n  - checks that no source content is persisted.\n- `failed_submission_can_be_confirmed_on_a_later_attempt` covers a failed request leaving `sent_in_request` null and a later retry setting it.\n- `throttled_requests_retry_unchanged_and_record_every_attempt` (`mod.rs`) covers 8 failed attempts followed by one success. Every input has `submitted_in_request == 0` and `sent_in_request == 8`.\n- `tool_outcomes_are_distinct_and_unknown_names_do_not_leak_model_text` covers `list_files` and `outline` as tool calls with no inputs, plus failures, skips and unknown tool names.\n- `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free` (`tests/authoring_cli.rs`) is an end-to-end run covering these points:\n  - a list, a search, a read, a repeat read and a failing read;\n  - `tool_failures.habeas == 1` and `tool_calls.habeas == 3`;\n  - a repeated habeas input with `unique == false` and `sent_in_request == 1`;\n  - mandatory definition inputs with `bytes > 0`;\n  - no `.` or `absent.txt` input in the evidence;\n  - no source or credential text in the usage record.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs` tests assert `listing.inputs.is_empty()` for `list_files`.\n\n**Minor test limits**\n- The end-to-end test does not assert a `requirements`-kind mandatory input or a `ripgrep` origin. Both go through the same `prepared` path, and the unit test covers the `requirements` kind and `components`.\n- No test covers a multi-byte character that spans the bytes calculation beyond the `λ` case. That case does exercise the UTF-8 rule.",
